\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch I was born August 27th, 1991. I was essentially unchurched my whole non-adult life. Christianity did not really enter the fabric of my life. People tried to convert me to various churches. On three occasion I attended my friends\'92 churches. It was fun, the churches smelled good, but that was all I got out of it. During high school (later) I ended up attending Mountain View Southern Baptist Church, going with the lady that drove my bus, three times. Then I caught the flu and wound up never returning, not for any particular reason. I wasn\'92t able to incorporate any bit of the experience into my life, and soon turned to paganism.}{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch About paganism I will say that I learned a lot, but it was a hollow experience, being without community and guidance. I was starting to feel out what I was looking for, but this wasn\'92t it, and I floundered spiritually.}{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch When I was 25 I met Rachel, first online, and then in real life when she drove to meet me and my family sight unseen in the middle of the redneckiest place she had ever seen, understanding that she is a hillbilly and not a redneck (they aren\'92t the same, she says). And during one of the myriad conversations that we had, she told me that she was a lapsed Catholic, which sparked a 5 hour long conversation (seriously) about the Catholic faith. During the course of that conversation I experienced my first real spiritual awakening. After this we went on to visit the Catholic church in Forest City. When I would go with Rachel to Bryson City, NC, I would visit St. Joseph\'92s there quite often. It was peaceful and I enjoyed the atmosphere of the church there. This is significant to me because it was really the first actual steps that I took after that initial spiritual awakening. }{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch Not until after the COVID epidemic would I begin to think about looking for a church congregation to join. Rachel insisted it would be a terrible bad no good idea to convert to Catholicism, because --- }{\s28\f0\fs24\i\lang1033{\*\listtag0}obviously}{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0} because we are both transgender. So I looked around with all that in mind and the natural answer and obvious choice was St. Francis Episcopal Church. The Episcopal Church had a reputation for being }{\s28\f0\fs24\i\lang1033{\*\listtag0}Catholic-lite}{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}, and that is actually exactly what I was looking for. I mean that as pure praise for the Episcopal Church, by the way. Also, and critically, the Episcopal Church has an accepting view toward LGBT folks. I learned all this online, and as much more as possible, before attending. The first Sunday of Lent 2023 I attended here for the first time. My first experience then is hard to put into words or explain. But it was wonderful. Everything, building, people, service --- it was all like having my eyes opened to what Christianity could, and should, be.}{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}
\pard\plain\ltrpar\ql\s28\itap0{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\abinodiroverride\ltrch It was July 2023 (I was thirty one at the time) when I was baptized. After that I would join in on a variety of church activities and roles within the church that I\'92ve been doing for going on three years now. I have found both a home and a family in worship and service to the church as a worship leader, in being a greeter, usher, and acolyte, and now a member of EfM as well. }{\s28\f0\fs24\lang1033{\*\listtag0}\par}}